START HERE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DELIVERY_NOTE.pdf

Read that first. It says what these models are, why there are three
files per model per origin, what the files contain, how the columns are
defined, and what is not included. @PAGECOUNT@ pages.

This file is a packing list and nothing more. Where it and the delivery
note disagree, the delivery note is correct.


WHAT IS IN THIS FOLDER
------------------------------------------------------------------------
One folder per model, @MODELCOUNT@ in all:

@MODELLIST@

Inside each model folder, three frame directories. They are the SAME
run collapsed three different ways, not three models and not three runs:

  I_mean/                 the mean of the 16 posterior draws. This is
                          the estimator the models declare.
  II_q95/                 the 95th percentile across those draws.
  III_conditional_mean/   the mean over the positive draws only,
                          E[y | y > 0].

Section @FRAMESECTION@ of the delivery note, "The Three Frames",
explains the three and says which of them can be reproduced.

Each frame directory holds @ORIGINCOUNT@ Parquet files, one per forecast
origin, named ..._00 upwards. They overlap in time and must not be
stacked into a panel; the note says why.

@FILECOUNT@ Parquet files in all, @BUNDLESIZE@. Every file holds @ROWCOUNT@
rows and five columns. Section @FILESECTION@ of the delivery note,
"The Delivered Files", names and defines the columns.


OPENING A FILE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Python
  import pandas as pd
  df = pd.read_parquet("purple_alien/I_mean/@SAMPLEFILE@")

R
  library(arrow)
  df <- read_parquet("purple_alien/I_mean/@SAMPLEFILE@")


CHECKING IT YOURSELF
------------------------------------------------------------------------
  audit_selector_frames.ipynb

That notebook audits this folder. Its outputs are saved from the run
that checked this exact copy of the data, so it can be read without
running anything. To re-run it, start Jupyter here and Run All; it
needs only pandas, pyarrow, numpy and matplotlib.

What it found, so you do not have to take it on trust: across all
@FILECOUNT@ files and all 2.18 billion delivered values, no missing,
negative or infinite value; every file a complete block with no
repeated cell-month; columns and types exact everywhere.

There is no checksum file and nothing else to verify by hand.


